# Force LF line endings for shell scripts regardless of the checking-out platform's
# core.autocrlf setting. Without this, a Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true is a very common
# default there) silently converts these to CRLF on disk - harmless until someone uploads that
# working-tree copy somewhere a shell actually has to run it (e.g. a GitHub release asset,
# fetched and piped straight into `sudo bash` by install_linux.sh's own documented one-liner),
# where CRLF breaks the shebang and produces `$'\r': command not found` / `invalid option`
# errors. Discovered exactly that way when testing the real published install_linux.sh.
*.sh text eol=lf
# GitHub Actions workflow YAML - same rationale, kept consistent for anything with embedded
# shell (`run:` blocks) even though a normal `actions/checkout` on a Linux runner is unaffected
# by this machine's autocrlf setting (only a manual out-of-band copy would ever hit this).
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
